{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 44",
  "problem": "Given an arithmetic sequence that has ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ what is the index of the term $90?$",
  "worked_solution": "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term ${a}_{1}.$\n\nSince we are given ${a}_{4}=13$ and ${a}_{8}=41,$ we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{4}}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{41-13}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{4} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{4}=13$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+3(7) \\\\ 13 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ -8 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-8+(n-1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+7n-7 \\\\  & =7n-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives ${a}_{n}=90,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =7n-15 \\\\ 90 & =7n-15 \\\\ 105 & =7n \\\\ \\frac{105}{7} & =n \\\\ 15 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $90$ is the $15$th term of the sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
